<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A NEW PROJECT FADES AT 3 ms; A FILE THAT STATES OTHERWISE WINS.

  Plan 53 P5 (QBX-183), decision D3a (a). The auto-fade is SEEDED into a
  project at the moment it is created and is NOT a read-default. Those are two
  different things and the difference is the whole reason D3a was a decision:

    - 3 ms as a READ-DEFAULT (option (b)) would be what every project ever
      saved reads, because no file written before plan 53 carries the key and
      `SProject`'s property load MERGES the file's keys over the seeded
      defaults. Every one of those projects would render differently than it
      does today, which moves every byte-identity golden and every per-second
      RMS gate in the suite.
    - 3 ms as a SEED is written once, into a project that has no file behind it
      yet. An old project is untouched because the file says so, and nothing
      has to remember which kind it is holding.

  So `SProjectProps::autoFadeMsDefault()` is ZERO and `autoFadeMsSeed()` is
  3.0, and the seed has exactly two call sites: `SMainWindow`'s fileNew path
  and `close-project`'s fresh project. Never `fileOpen`. It is the rule
  `srecordmodeseed.h` states at length for the record mode, and this case is
  `record_mode_ini_seed.qxa`'s shape for the auto-fade.

  WHY `close-project` IS THE INSTRUMENT. The runner's own `setup project="new"`
  does NOT seed — it builds an `SProject` directly — so a scripted project
  starts at 0, which is what keeps every case in this suite that predates plan
  53 rendering exactly as it always did (and is itself asserted, from the other
  side, by `auto_fade_seam.qxa`: its first render is of a fresh scripted
  project and must be the UNFADED one). `close-project` instead builds its
  replacement through the REAL new-project path, so it is the only way a .qxa
  can reach the seed at all.

  WHAT THIS CASE CANNOT PROVE, measured and not assumed, exactly as
  `record_mode_ini_seed.qxa` records for its own key:
  `SLoadProjectAction::apply` loads INTO THE CURRENT PROJECT. It clears the
  arrangements and NOT the properties, so an in-memory `autoFadeMs` survives a
  `load-project` whatever the file holds. `fileOpen()` does not behave that way
  — it loads into a fresh `SProject` — so no .qxa can reach fileOpen's
  semantics, and a case that loaded a file with NO key and asserted 0 would be
  asserting the verb's merge rather than the seed's placement.

  So this case gates the SUBSTANCE, the way that one does: a file that STATES a
  value wins over the seed. An implementation that seeded after the merge, on
  the load path, would read 3 ms below and fail.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="auto_fade_seed">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <snap-to-grid-disable/>

    <!-- A FRESH PROJECT THROUGH THE REAL NEW-PROJECT PATH. This is where the
         seed runs, and the clips below are placed into the seeded project. -->
    <close-project/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_stereo.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="144000"/>

    <render filename="sd_seeded.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="3.5"/>

    <!-- ...and the same project with the auto-fade explicitly off, which is
         the comparison that says the seed is 3 ms and not 0. -->
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[0.0]"/>
    <render filename="sd_zero.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="3.5"/>

    <!-- THE SEEDED PROJECT FADED AT BOTH EDGES, 144 frames each... -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="sd_seeded.wav" expected="sd_zero.wav"
                           startFrame="0" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="sd_seeded.wav" expected="sd_zero.wav"
                           startFrame="143856" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <!-- ...and NOWHERE ELSE, which is what pins the seeded LENGTH: a seed of
         6 ms would move frames inside this range. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="sd_seeded.wav" expected="sd_zero.wav"
                           startFrame="144" frameCount="143712"/>

    <!-- A FILE THAT STATES A VALUE WINS OVER THE SEED. The project is at 0
         now, so the file states 0. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/auto_fade_seed/afs.qxp"/>

    <close-project/>
    <load-project path="../../build/test-output/auto_fade_seed/afs.qxp"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="144000"/>
    <render filename="sd_reload.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="3.5"/>

    <!-- The loaded project renders the UNFADED file, byte for byte, although
         the project it was loaded into had just been seeded at 3 ms. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="sd_reload.wav" expected="sd_zero.wav"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
